export default "# Issue: Make /workflow-branch model-invocable, with triggers and a first-question gate\n\n**Source**: DW-4 (manual description)\n**Source type**: manual\n**Parent**: none\n**Additional context**: none\n**Date**: 2026-08-26\n**Branch**: feature/DW-4_model-invocable-branch-skill\n**Base branch**: main\n**Status**: Clarified \u2014 awaiting planning\n\n## Problem Statement\n\n`/workflow-branch` can only be reached by typing it. Like all 18 skills in this plugin it\ncarries `disable-model-invocation: true` (`skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md:6`), so Claude can\nnever invoke it \u2014 even when the developer has plainly asked for a branch. The task is to make\nthis one skill an exception and give Claude the signals it needs to fire it at the right moments.\n\nTwo halves, and the second is the larger one:\n\n**1. The flag is only half the mechanism.** Flipping `disable-model-invocation` makes the skill\n*eligible* for autonomous invocation; it does not make it *discoverable*. The `description`\nfield is the only text Claude reads when deciding whether a skill applies, and this skill's\ndescription says what it does \u2014 \"cut a working branch for the current task from a verified base,\nnamed after the repo's own convention\" \u2014 not when it applies. A skill that is eligible but has\nno trigger language is, in practice, still never invoked. Anti-triggers matter as much as\ntriggers: nothing here should fire on \"what branch am I on\", \"list the branches\", or a branch\nname mentioned in passing.\n\n**2. The skill has no \"do you want a branch?\" gate \u2014 and today it does not need one.** Surfaced\nwhile scoping this issue. Read end to end, every ask in the skill already presumes a branch is\nbeing cut:\n\n- \u00a71 asks only *conditionally* \u2014 when a branch for the key already exists (`:103-115`), it\n  offers staying put or switching to it.\n- \u00a72 (`:117`) asks which base, which presumes the cut.\n- \u00a73 (`:145`) asks about prefix and name, same presumption.\n- \u00a75 (`:282`) offers \"abandon the branch\", but only when the base is not HEAD.\n- \u00a76 (`:288`) cuts it.\n\nOn the common path \u2014 clean tree, no existing branch for the key, base is HEAD \u2014 the skill runs\nbase menu \u2192 name menu \u2192 `git switch -c` and never asks whether branching was wanted at all.\n\nThat is safe *today* precisely because the skill is not model-invocable: typing\n`/workflow-branch` is itself the consent. It stops being safe the moment Claude can start the\nskill on its own, which is what this issue does. The approval that appears to exist is model\nbehaviour \u2014 narrating and pausing \u2014 not a skill instruction, which is the same shape as the\nDW-3 commit-message gate: reliable right up until the conditions that produced it change.\n\nSo this issue has to land both halves together. Making the skill model-invocable without adding\nthe gate would let Claude cut branches off an inferred intent with no point at which a developer\ncan say \"no, not now\".\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\n**Invocation.** Claude invokes `/workflow-branch` on its own in three situations:\n\n- **Explicit branch intent** \u2014 \"create a branch\", \"cut a branch for DW-5\", \"I need a branch off\n  develop\".\n- **Start of work on a keyed ticket while HEAD is on a base branch** \u2014 \"let's implement DW-5\",\n  \"start on PROJ-123\" while sitting on `main` or `develop`.\n- **Immediately after `/workflow-clarify` writes a fresh scratchpad**, as a handoff rather than\n  the closing suggestion it is today.\n\nIt does **not** fire on read-only branch questions, on branch names mentioned in passing, or\nwhen HEAD already carries the issue's key.\n\n**Behaviour once running.** An autonomous run is identical to a typed one \u2014 same base menu, same\nprefix table, same name confirmation. Nothing is cut without a human choosing the name and base.\n\n**The new first gate.** After \u00a71 has gathered context (key, parent, existing-branch scan) and\nbefore \u00a72 offers a base, the skill asks once, unconditionally \u2014 on typed and autonomous runs\nalike \u2014 presenting a decision menu rather than a yes/no:\n\n```\nDW-4 \u2014 \"make workflow-branch model-invocable\"\nNo existing branch carries this key.\n\n  1. Cut a new branch          (continues to base + name)\n  2. Stay on main\n```\n\nWhen the \u00a71 scan finds a branch for the key, that finding becomes a row in the same menu \u2014\n`2. Switch to feature/DW-4_\u2026 (3 behind HEAD)` \u2014 rather than a separate conditional path. This\nabsorbs \u00a71's two existing conditional asks (`:103-115`) into one gate instead of adding a fourth\nquestion to the skill.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` frontmatter reads `disable-model-invocation: false` \u2014\n      the key is kept and the value flipped, not removed\n- [ ] The other 17 skills still carry `disable-model-invocation: true`, unchanged\n- [ ] `model: sonnet` and `argument-hint:` are left in place on `workflow-branch` (deliberate\n      non-spec keys, per project notes \u2014 not to be \"fixed\" for the validator)\n- [ ] The `description` field carries explicit trigger language covering all three situations:\n      explicit branch intent, start-of-work on a keyed ticket from a base branch, and the\n      post-clarify handoff\n- [ ] The `description` also carries anti-triggers: read-only branch questions, a branch name\n      mentioned in passing, and HEAD already on a branch carrying the key\n- [ ] A new unconditional gate sits between \u00a71 and \u00a72, before any base is offered\n- [ ] The gate is a decision menu, not a yes/no, and always includes a \"stay on `<current>`\" row\n- [ ] When \u00a71's scan finds a branch for the key, that branch appears as a row in the gate, and\n      \u00a71's existing conditional asks (`:103-115`) are folded into it rather than left duplicated\n- [ ] The gate fires on typed `/workflow-branch` invocations too \u2014 no invocation-mode-dependent\n      behaviour, since a SKILL.md cannot reliably detect how it was invoked\n- [ ] `skills/workflow-clarify/SKILL.md` is **not** edited \u2014 the chaining lives entirely in\n      `workflow-branch`'s description\n- [ ] Given a clean tree on `main` and no branch for the key, when the skill runs, then it\n      reaches the gate before running any `git fetch` or `git switch`\n- [ ] Given the developer picks \"stay on `<current>`\", when the gate resolves, then no branch is\n      created, no base is fast-forwarded, and the scratchpad's `**Branch**` / `**Base branch**`\n      fields are left untouched\n- [ ] Section numbering stays contiguous after the insertion, and every cross-reference to a\n      section number elsewhere in the file still resolves\n- [ ] `README.md:37` and `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` (lines 50, 90, 151, 200) reflect that\n      this skill can now be invoked by Claude, and that it opens with the gate\n- [ ] `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` version is bumped\n- [ ] `git log` and a fresh session confirm the skill is offered autonomously on at least one\n      trigger phrase without the command being typed\n\n## Affected Components\n\n- `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 the whole of the change: frontmatter flag, rewritten\n  `description` with triggers and anti-triggers, and the new gate inserted between \u00a71 and \u00a72\n  with \u00a71's conditional asks folded into it\n- `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 four references (lines 50, 90, 151, 200). Line 151's advice\n  (\"Run /workflow-branch right after clarify\") and line 50's \"Suggests /workflow-branch when it's\n  done\" both describe a handoff that is changing; line 200's table row describes the skill's\n  inputs and outputs\n- `README.md:37` \u2014 the one-line description of the command, which currently frames it as\n  something you run\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 version bump. Its `description` also claims \"Each step is an\n  explicit /workflow-\\* skill\", which is no longer strictly true once one skill is autonomous.\n  Note the root `plugin.json` (Agent Plugins manifest) is **absent on `main`** \u2014 it arrives with\n  DW-2 \u2014 so only one manifest exists to edit on this branch\n- `CLAUDE.local.md` (personal notes, not checked in) \u2014 records \"Most steps set\n  `disable-model-invocation: true` on purpose \u2014 the user drives each step explicitly. Don't\n  remove it without asking\" and cites `/workflow-release` firing autonomously as the risk. That\n  rationale needs updating to record `workflow-branch` as a deliberate, asked-for exception, or\n  the next session will read the change as a mistake\n- `CHANGELOG.md` \u2014 new entry at release time\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Scenarios\n\n- **Not a git repository** \u2014 the skill already stops with a one-line note (`:26`). The gate must\n  sit after that check, not before it, or it asks about branching in a directory that cannot\n  branch\n- **HEAD already on a branch carrying the key** \u2014 must be an anti-trigger *and* handled at the\n  gate. Autonomous invocation here is pure noise: the developer is already where they need to be\n- **The gate's \"stay put\" row must be a true no-op** \u2014 no `git fetch origin`, no\n  `git fetch origin <base>:<base>` moving a local ref, no scratchpad write. \u00a74's base refresh\n  mutates local refs and currently runs unconditionally once the skill is past \u00a73\n- **Dirty working tree** \u2014 \u00a75's stash/commit/abandon question now sits behind the gate.\n  Confirm the two do not ask overlapping questions: \"abandon the branch\" at \u00a75 and \"stay on\n  `<current>`\" at the gate are the same answer arriving at different points\n- **Autonomous fire mid-task** \u2014 Claude notices \"let's implement DW-5\" while the developer is\n  deep in unrelated work with a dirty tree. The gate is the whole defence here\n- **Chained fire after clarify when clarify found no key** \u2014 \u00a73 already handles a missing key by\n  asking for one (`:214`); the gate must not present a menu headed by an empty key\n- **Double-firing** \u2014 clarify ends, Claude fires the skill via the description trigger, the\n  developer also types `/workflow-branch`. Second run hits \u00a71's existing-branch scan and the gate\n  should show the just-created branch as a \"switch to it\" row\n- **Over-firing on ambiguous phrasing** \u2014 \"I'll branch off that idea\" or \"which branch has the\n  fix\" must not trigger. This is the main risk the anti-trigger language exists to contain, and\n  it cannot be tested except by use\n- **Trigger text costs context in every session** \u2014 the `description` of every skill is loaded\n  into every session's skill listing. A long TRIGGER/SKIP block buys precision and is paid for\n  continuously; keep it as tight as the three situations allow\n\n## Dependencies & Blockers\n\n- **No blockers.** No code, no build, no tests \u2014 the change is markdown prompts, and the skill\n  auto-loads from `~/.claude/skills/` in any new session with no install step\n- **Version-number contention with DW-2** \u2014 the manifest is at `1.7.2` on `main`. DW-2\n  (`feature/DW-2_agent-plugins-compatibility`, unmerged) already claimed `1.8.0`. This change is\n  a new capability, which points at a minor bump and therefore straight at that collision.\n  Decide the number at plan time, as DW-3 did\n- **DW-2 merge overlap** \u2014 DW-2 also edits `README.md` and the `version` line of\n  `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`, and adds the root `plugin.json`. Both touch points recur here, so\n  expect a conflict whenever DW-2 merges. Its root manifest carries the same `description` field\n  that this change makes stale, and the six duplicated manifest fields must be kept identical\n- **Verification is manual** \u2014 nothing in this repo can test that a trigger fires. Confirming the\n  skill is offered autonomously requires a fresh session and a real prompt\n\n## Open Questions\n\nAll four resolved during clarification:\n\n- **How broadly may Claude fire the skill?** \u2192 **Broadest.** Explicit branch intent, plus\n  start-of-work signals on a keyed ticket from a base branch, plus chaining after\n  `/workflow-clarify`. Noted tension: this contradicts the \"user drives each step explicitly\"\n  design note, which is why that note needs updating rather than quietly contradicting the code\n- **May an autonomous run actually create the branch?** \u2192 **Yes, menus intact** \u2014 and this\n  question is what surfaced the missing gate. Resolved by the next answer\n- **Where does the gate sit, and is it unconditional?** \u2192 **After \u00a71, unconditional.** Gather\n  context first so the question is informed, then ask once as a decision menu. Unconditional\n  because a SKILL.md cannot reliably detect its own invocation mode \u2014 an empty `$ARGUMENTS` is\n  matched by a bare typed `/workflow-branch` just as well as by an autonomous fire, so\n  \"ask only when model-invoked\" is not cleanly implementable\n- **How is post-clarify chaining wired?** \u2192 **In `workflow-branch`'s description only.**\n  `workflow-clarify` is not edited. Weaker guarantee than a hard-wired handoff, but a missed\n  chain costs only the suggestion clarify already prints, and it keeps the change to one\n  load-bearing prompt\n- **How is the frontmatter written?** \u2192 **`disable-model-invocation: false`**, key kept and value\n  flipped. Behaviourally identical to removing it, but it keeps the exception visible against the\n  seventeen `true`s so it does not read as an oversight\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n**Mode**: Regular\n**Complexity**: Medium\n**Steps**: 8\n\n### Placement decision (settled before Step 1)\n\nClarify resolved the gate to \"after \u00a71, before \u00a72\". There are two ways to write that, and they\nare not equally safe:\n\n- **A new numbered `## 2. Decide whether to branch`** renumbers \u00a72\u2013\u00a76 into \u00a73\u2013\u00a77. The file\n  carries eight step-number cross-references (`:35`, `:70`, `:106`, `:119`, `:200`, `:214`,\n  `:235`, `:271`); six of them point at sections \u22652 and would every one become wrong. Nothing in\n  this repo would catch that \u2014 the file is a prompt, and a reference reading \"go to step 5\" that\n  now means step 6 misroutes the model silently.\n- **A closing `### Decide whether to branch` subsection inside \u00a71**, with \u00a71 retitled, lands the\n  gate at exactly the same point in execution \u2014 after the scan, immediately before the base menu\n  \u2014 and renumbers nothing.\n\nThis plan takes the second. It is also the better semantic fit: the gate consumes \u00a71's findings\n(key, parent, existing branches), so it belongs with them rather than standing apart from them.\n\n### Step 1: Flip the frontmatter flag\n- **Action**: Change `disable-model-invocation: true` to `false`. Keep the key, keep `model:\n  sonnet` and `argument-hint:` untouched.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify (line 6)\n- **Verify**: `grep -c 'disable-model-invocation: true' skills/*/SKILL.md` totals 17, and\n  `workflow-branch` is the one file reporting `false`.\n\n### Step 2: Rewrite the `description` to carry triggers and anti-triggers\n- **Action**: Extend the existing description with an explicit TRIGGER / SKIP block covering the\n  three agreed situations (explicit branch intent; start of work on a keyed ticket while HEAD is\n  on a base branch; right after `/workflow-clarify` writes a fresh scratchpad) and the three\n  anti-triggers (read-only branch questions, a branch name in passing, HEAD already carrying the\n  key). Keep the existing \"cut a working branch \u2026 repo's own convention\" sentence as the opener \u2014\n  it is what the help table and README quote.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify (line 3)\n- **Verify**: Frontmatter still parses as valid YAML on one line; the post-clarify trigger is\n  present here and **nowhere in `workflow-clarify`**.\n\n### Step 3: Retitle \u00a71 and add the gate as its closing subsection\n- **Action**: Retitle `## 1. Gather context` \u2192 `## 1. Gather context and confirm intent`. Append\n  a final `### Decide whether to branch` subsection presenting a decision menu, not a yes/no:\n  a \"cut a new branch\" row, a \"stay on `<current>`\" row that is always present, and one row per\n  branch the scan turned up. State that it runs unconditionally \u2014 on typed and autonomous\n  invocations alike \u2014 with the reason: a SKILL.md cannot detect its own invocation mode, since a\n  bare typed `/workflow-branch` presents exactly the same empty `$ARGUMENTS` as an autonomous\n  fire.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify (after line 115)\n- **Verify**: The gate sits after all three \u00a71 subsections and before `## 2. Choose the base\n  branch`; section numbering is untouched; all eight step-number cross-references still resolve.\n\n### Step 4: Fold \u00a71's conditional asks into the gate\n- **Action**: Rewrite the \"HEAD is already on it\" / \"It exists elsewhere\" bullets (`:103-115`) so\n  the scan *reports* what it found and the gate *owns* the decision. Keep the\n  `git rev-list --left-right --count HEAD...<branch>` reading \u2014 it becomes the annotation on that\n  branch's row. Same for the parent-story search below it, which currently has its own competing\n  \"offer switching first\" instruction.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify (lines 103\u2013115)\n- **Verify**: Exactly one place in the file asks whether to branch. No path reaches \u00a72 without\n  passing the gate, and the parent-story recommendation survives as a row ordering rather than a\n  second question.\n\n### Step 5: Make \"stay put\" a true no-op\n- **Action**: State explicitly at the gate that choosing to stay ends the skill: no `git fetch\n  origin`, no `git fetch origin <base>:<base>` (which moves a local ref), no `git switch`, and no\n  scratchpad write. Add a line to \u00a75's stash/commit/abandon question noting that \"abandon the\n  branch\" and the gate's \"stay put\" are the same answer arriving late, so the two do not both get\n  asked.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify (gate block, and `:274-283`)\n- **Verify**: Trace the \"stay\" path end to end \u2014 it terminates inside \u00a71 and touches no ref and\n  no file.\n\n### Step 6: Update `workflow-help`\n- **Action**: Four edits. Line 50 (\"Suggests /workflow-branch when it's done\") \u2014 the handoff is\n  now a trigger, not just a suggestion. Lines 90\u201394 (utility entry) \u2014 note it can be invoked by\n  Claude and opens with the branch/stay decision. Line 151 (the \"run it right after clarify\" tip)\n  \u2014 reword now that it can fire on its own. Line 200 (step table row) \u2014 the `Reads`/`Writes`\n  columns still hold; add that it is the one model-invocable skill.\n- **Files**: `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: `grep -n 'workflow-branch' skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` returns the same four\n  locations, each consistent with the new behaviour.\n\n### Step 7: Update `README.md` and the manifest\n- **Action**: Rewrite the `/workflow-branch` bullet at `README.md:37` to say it can be invoked by\n  Claude and that it asks before cutting. Bump `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`. Its `description`\n  claims \"Each step is an explicit /workflow-\\* skill\", which this change makes inaccurate \u2014\n  soften it in the same pass. Root `plugin.json` is absent on this branch; do not create it.\n  Bump target is **`1.7.3`** (patch), decided at plan confirmation.\n- **Files**: `README.md` \u2014 modify; `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: `grep -h '\"version\"' .claude-plugin/plugin.json` shows `1.7.3`; JSON parses;\n  no root `plugin.json` was created.\n\n### Step 8: Record the exception in the project notes\n- **Action**: `CLAUDE.local.md` states that skills set `disable-model-invocation: true` on\n  purpose and names `/workflow-release` firing autonomously as the risk. Amend it to record\n  `workflow-branch` as a deliberate exception, asked for in DW-4, with the gate as the mitigation.\n- **Files**: `CLAUDE.local.md` \u2014 modify\n- **Verify**: A reader landing on the `false` in step 1 finds the reason without needing this\n  scratchpad. (Confirm whether the file is tracked before the commit step \u2014 if gitignored, it is\n  still worth updating, just not committed.)\n\n### Risks & Unknowns\n\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Renumbering is the trap this plan is built to avoid.** Six live cross-references break if\n  the gate becomes a numbered section. If implementation drifts back to that shape, all six must\n  move with it.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Over-firing cannot be tested here.** \"I'll branch off that idea\", \"which branch has the\n  fix\" \u2014 the anti-trigger wording is the only defence, and nothing in this repo can exercise it.\n  Real use is the test, and the cost of getting it wrong is an unwanted menu, not a lost branch:\n  the gate contains it.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Version collision with DW-2.** DW-2 claimed `1.8.0` and is unmerged; this is a new\n  capability, which points at the same number. Whichever merges second has to move. Deciding here\n  makes the conflict a one-line merge conflict rather than a release-time surprise.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **`description` is loaded in every session, for every skill.** The trigger block is paid for\n  continuously, not once. Keep it to the three situations and three anti-triggers; resist growing\n  it into prose.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **DW-2 merge overlap.** Both branches edit `README.md` and the manifest `version` line.\n  DW-2's root `plugin.json` carries the same `description` this step softens, so that fix has to\n  be applied there too when DW-2 lands \u2014 the six duplicated manifest fields must stay identical.\n- \u2705 **Bump number \u2014 decided: `1.7.3` (patch).** Chosen over `1.8.0` to keep clear of DW-2's\n  claim, so nothing has to move when that branch merges. Accepts the same tension DW-3 carried:\n  a change that adds a capability ships under a patch number. Worth remembering at release, where\n  the changelog will describe more than the version implies.\n- \u2753 **Is `CLAUDE.local.md` tracked?** Determines whether step 8 is part of the commit or a purely\n  local edit. Check at implementation time, not now \u2014 this step is read-only.\n\n### Dependencies\n\n- None blocking. No build, no tests, no install: the skill auto-loads from `~/.claude/skills/`,\n  so step 1 takes effect in the next session.\n- Steps 1\u20135 are one file and should land together; a flipped flag without the gate is the one\n  intermediate state worth not committing.\n- Steps 6\u20138 are documentation and can follow in the same commit.\n- Verification that a trigger actually fires needs a fresh session and a real prompt \u2014 it cannot\n  happen inside this one.\n\n## Implementation Status\n\n**Completed**: 2026-08-26\n**Steps completed**: 8 of 8\n**Blocked steps**: none\n\n### Changes Made\n\n- `skills/workflow-branch/SKILL.md` \u2014 `disable-model-invocation` flipped to `false` (key kept, so\n  the exception stays visible against the other seventeen). `description` extended with a\n  TRIGGER/SKIP block covering the three agreed triggers and three anti-triggers; single quotes\n  used inside the double-quoted scalar so nothing needs escaping. \u00a71 retitled to \"Gather context\n  and confirm intent\" and given a closing `### Decide whether to branch` subsection: an\n  unconditional decision menu with the parent-story branch first, existing key-carrying branches\n  next, \"cut a new branch\", and \"stay on `<current>`\" always last. The former conditional asks at\n  `:103-115` now only *record* findings that the gate consumes. \"Staying put ends the skill\" is\n  stated explicitly \u2014 no fetch, no ref move, no switch, no scratchpad write. \u00a75 gained a\n  paragraph so a late \"abandon the branch\" is treated as the gate's answer arriving late rather\n  than a second question.\n- `skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2014 four references updated: clarify now \"hands off to\"\n  `/workflow-branch`; the utility entry gained five lines describing autonomous invocation and\n  the gate; the tip reworded to \"usually follows clarify on its own \u2026 run it yourself any time it\n  doesn't\"; the step-table Purpose cell notes it is the one model-invocable skill.\n- `README.md` \u2014 the `/workflow-branch` bullet now states it is the only workflow skill Claude can\n  invoke on its own, names the three triggers, and says it always asks before cutting.\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 version `1.7.2` \u2192 `1.7.3`. The `description`'s \"Each step is an\n  explicit /workflow-\\* skill\" became inaccurate with this change and was softened to \"Each step\n  is a /workflow-\\* skill you invoke explicitly, except branch creation which can also be offered\n  automatically\".\n- `CLAUDE.local.md` \u2014 records `workflow-branch` as the one deliberate exception, why the key was\n  set to `false` rather than deleted, and that the gate is what makes autonomous invocation safe.\n  Gitignored via `*.local*`, so this edit is local-only and will not appear in the commit.\n\n### Deviations from the plan\n\n- **Gate placement.** The plan already called this: it went in as a closing subsection of \u00a71\n  rather than a new numbered section. Confirmed after the fact \u2014 headings still run `## 1.`\n  through `## 6.`, and all eight `step N` cross-references still resolve. A numbered insert would\n  have invalidated six of them silently.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `disable-model-invocation: true` in exactly 17 skills; `false` in `workflow-branch` alone.\n- Frontmatter parses as five clean key/value lines; `description` is 566 chars with no stray or\n  unescaped quotes.\n- Section headings run 1\u20136 unbroken, gate at `:118`, before `## 2.` at `:150`.\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` parses as JSON and reports `1.7.3`; root `plugin.json` still\n  absent, as intended on this branch.\n- `grep -c 'workflow-branch' skills/workflow-help/SKILL.md` \u2192 4, unchanged.\n- **Live**: the skill appeared in this session's model-invocable skill listing immediately after\n  the edit, carrying the new description \u2014 the flag and trigger text are in effect without a\n  restart.\n\n### Issues Encountered\n\n- Frontmatter could not be validated with a real YAML parser (`python3` has no `yaml` module\n  here), so it was checked structurally instead \u2014 line count, quote balance, no embedded\n  delimiters. The skill listing then loaded it successfully, which is the stronger signal.\n- **Untestable by design**: over-firing on ambiguous phrasing (\"I'll branch off that idea\",\n  \"which branch has the fix\") cannot be exercised from inside this repo. The anti-trigger wording\n  is the only defence, and real use is the test. The gate bounds the cost of getting it wrong to\n  an unwanted menu.\n\n## Release\n\n**Version**: 1.7.3\n**Date**: 2026-08-26\n**Tag**: v1.7.3\n**Pushed**: no \u2014 `main` is 2 commits ahead of `origin/main`, and the tag is local only\n\n### Commit\n\n- `7450dda` \u2014 chore(DW-4): release v1.7.3 \u2014 make branch skill model-invocable (tagged v1.7.3)\n- `faa2100` \u2014 feat(DW-4): make workflow-branch model-invocable with a branch gate\n\n### Notes\n\n- Released from `main`, which was fast-forwarded from\n  `feature/DW-4_model-invocable-branch-skill` before this step.\n- The bump is a patch by choice, not by SemVer: `faa2100` is a `feat`, and the changelog entry\n  describes a new capability plus a new gate. `1.7.3` was taken to leave `1.8.0` free for DW-2.\n- DW-2 (`feature/DW-2_agent-plugins-compatibility`, `d25d4b8`) is now three commits behind `main`\n  and still unmerged. It adds the root `plugin.json`, whose `description` must be kept identical\n  to the one in `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` \u2014 and that field changed in this release, so the\n  copy DW-2 brings will be stale on arrival.\n"
